hostname r3
!
ip routing
!
! -------------------------------------------------------------------
! r3 — ABR between Area 1 (transit) and Area 0 (backbone)
!
! r3 has interfaces in:
!   - Area 1: the transit area connecting to r2
!   - Area 0: the backbone area connecting to r4
!
! r3 is the other endpoint of the virtual link with r2.
! Together they form the logical area 0 extension through area 1.
!
! The virtual link configuration on r3:
!   area 1 virtual-link 10.0.0.2
!   (10.0.0.2 is r2's router-id — the other end of the virtual link)
!
! Both endpoints MUST:
!   1. Be ABRs (have interfaces in the transit area AND area 0/virtual)
!   2. Share the same transit area (area 1 in this lab)
!   3. Be reachable from each other within the transit area
!
! Ethernet1: connects to r2 (10.1.23.0/30, r3=.2) — Area 1 (transit)
! Ethernet2: connects to r4 (10.1.34.0/30, r3=.1) — Area 0
! Loopback0:   loopback 10.0.0.3/32 — Area 0
! -------------------------------------------------------------------

interface Loopback0
 ip address 10.0.0.3/32

interface Ethernet1
 no switchport
 description to-r2 (OSPF area 1, transit for virtual link)
 ip address 10.1.23.2/30

interface Ethernet2
 no switchport
 description to-r4 (OSPF area 0)
 ip address 10.1.34.1/30

! -------------------------------------------------------------------
! TASK: Configure OSPF on r3 (ABR: area 1 and area 0)
!
! Step 1 — Basic OSPF without virtual link:
!
!   router ospf 1
!    router-id 10.0.0.3
!    network 10.0.0.3/32 area 0
!    network 10.1.23.0/30 area 1
!    network 10.1.34.0/30 area 0
!
!   Note: loopback in area 0. r3 is a real area 0 router.
!
!   Verify (before virtual link):
!     show ip ospf neighbor     <- r2 (area 1) and r4 (area 0)
!     show ip route ospf       <- area 0 and area 1 routes, but NOT area 2
!
! Step 2 — Add virtual link to r2 (through area 1):
!
!   router ospf 1
!    area 1 virtual-link 10.0.0.2
!
!   r3 must also configure the virtual link — both ends must match.
!
!   Verify:
!     show ip ospf virtual-links
!     -> "Virtual Link OSPF_VL0 to router 10.0.0.2" state "Point-to-Point"
!
!   After virtual link up:
!     show ip ospf neighbor
!     -> r2 appears twice: as area 1 physical neighbor AND virtual-link peer
!
!     show ip route ospf
!     -> O IA routes to area 2 (10.1.12.0/30, 10.0.0.1/32) now visible
!
! Virtual link transport:
!   OSPF Hello/DBD/LSR/LSU/LSAck for the virtual link are encapsulated
!   in OSPF unicast packets sent through area 1.
!   The virtual link behaves like an unnumbered point-to-point link in area 0.
! -------------------------------------------------------------------
